+# Privacy and deployment audit
+
+The release is a static site with no browser JavaScript, analytics, third-party
+requests, cookies, browser storage, personal-data forms, or application database.
+No consent preference or identifier needs to be stored. The privacy notice is at
+`/privacy-policy/`. Existing account, rewards, ordering, and contact links are
+placeholders for services outside this repository, not implemented data flows.
+The old GET logout link was removed: a static page has no session to terminate,
+and a future authenticated logout must use a server-validated POST with CSRF
+protection, not a state-changing navigation request.
+
+## Third-party inventory
+
+| Resource | Finding and decision |
+| --- | --- |
+| Unsplash hero photograph | Previously requested directly from `images.unsplash.com`, exposing visitors' connection metadata. Copied to `assets/coffee.jpg`; no visitor request to Unsplash remains. Original: https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1350&q=80 |
+| Fonts | System fonts only; no font service. |
+| Scripts, analytics, embeds, payments | None loaded or implemented. CSP disables scripts, connections, frames, workers, and form submissions. |
+| Hosting | Receives IP addresses and request metadata to serve files. Provider, log retention, and operator access are deployment-specific and cannot be audited from this repository. |
+| Build | Privacy audit and build use Node built-ins only, with no package downloads. The existing `start` convenience command uses a third-party static server; it is not the production deployment configuration and was not run. |
+
+## Enforced controls
+
+`npm run audit:privacy` checks the source inventory, local resource references,
+absence of browser scripts and collection markup, and required policy headers.
+`npm run build` runs that audit and copies only reviewed public files into
+`dist/`. Documentation, build scripts, and package metadata are not published.
+This is a guard against accidental regressions, not an independent penetration
+test or a substitute for reviewing newly added functionality.
+
+`netlify.toml` configures the build and publish directory. Netlify applies the
+included `_headers` to all routes. Other hosts must explicitly translate these
+rules into actual response headers; merely serving `_headers` as a file does
+not enforce them. The existing local static server is not configured to apply
+these headers. HTML provides CSP and no-referrer fallbacks, but framing rules,
+permissions restrictions, HSTS, and other response-only controls require host
+support. MDN documents that [frame-ancestors cannot be set in a meta element](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy/frame-ancestors).
+
+Use HTTPS and redirect HTTP to HTTPS at the host. HSTS covers this host for one
+year after a secure response; subdomains and preload are deliberately excluded
+until their HTTPS readiness is known. Responses use `no-store` to minimize HTTP
+caching; this does not erase browser history or hosting logs. CSP deliberately
+does not send violation reports, which could otherwise expose page URLs.
+
+## Secure handling for future services
+
+Before enabling accounts, ordering, rewards, or collection, review the purpose
+and minimum fields needed; avoid dates of birth, addresses, or identifiers unless
+the feature requires them. Publish the actual retention period, deletion route,
+and privacy contact. Keep optional marketing separate and off by default. Never
+put personal information, secrets, or authentication tokens in URLs, HTML,
+analytics, browser storage, or logs.
+
+An authenticated backend must validate and authorize every operation server-side,
+use TLS and Secure/HttpOnly/SameSite session cookies, protect state changes from
+CSRF, and keep sensitive responses out of caches. Store only necessary records
+with restricted access, redact logs, and implement retention and deletion in the
+backend. These are future integration requirements, not capabilities of this
+static site. Any policy relaxation requires a reviewed data-flow change.
+
+Before production release, the hosting operator must establish log minimization,
+retention, access restrictions, and a real privacy contact, then confirm the
+configured response headers on the chosen hosting platform. No live deployment
+or runtime header verification is claimed by the source audit.
